// ECDSA verification on a short-Weierstrass curve y^2 = x^3 - 3x + b over a prime field (RFC 6090 /
// FIPS 186-4), in neant, on the general modular arithmetic in src/neant/crypto/bignum.nt and on
// der.nt for the signature's encoding. Not part of the boot image — load all three:
//   load "src/neant/crypto/bignum.nt"   load "src/neant/crypto/der.nt"
//   load "src/neant/crypto/ec.nt"
// ct.nt it loads itself, because ecCtMul at the bottom is built out of that file's masks and a
// caller should not be able to get the signing path without them.
// and then one curve: src/neant/crypto/p256.nt or src/neant/crypto/p384.nt, which is what a caller
// actually names. Nothing here knows which curve it is on.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. It was p256.nt, and it was one curve. The public web's intermediates are
// ecdsa-with-SHA384 over P-384, so a second curve was needed; and because every value below is a
// bignum.nt limb list rather than a fixed-width P-256 word, the arithmetic needed nothing
// curve-specific to become general. Every function here takes the curve record `C` as its first
// argument, and the only difference between P-256 and P-384 in this file is the contents of that
// record — not one line of the group law, the ladder or the checks is written twice. p256.nt's
// exported names are still the ones tests/p256.nt and verify.nt call; they are thin wrappers now.
//
//   ecCurve[(p;n;b;gx;gy;dgst)] -> C   the domain parameters as hex strings, plus the digest
//                                      length in octets this curve's signatures are verified with
//   ecdsaVerify[C;pub;digest;r;s]      -> 1b / 0b
//   ecdsaVerifyDer[C;pub;digest;sig]   -> 1b / 0b   sig is DER SEQUENCE { INTEGER r, INTEGER s },
//                                                   which is what a certificate's signatureValue
//                                                   and a TLS CertificateVerify carry
//
// `pub` is the SEC 1 point x509.nt's `spki`point hands you; r and s are big-endian magnitudes.
// `digest` is the already-computed hash of the signed message and must be exactly `C`dgst` octets:
// a curve here is paired with ONE hash — P-256 with SHA-256, P-384 with SHA-384 — so a digest of
// any other length is refused rather than truncated or left-padded into something that would
// verify against the wrong hash. FIPS 186-4 6.4 does define a truncation (the leftmost
// min(bitlen(hash), bitlen(n)) bits) for a mismatched pair, and it is deliberately not implemented:
// it is one more thing to get subtly wrong in a verifier whose job is to say no, and the pairings
// above are the only ones certificates in the wild actually use.
//
// Both verifiers are total, exactly like rsaVerifyPkcs1: every malformed input — a point that is
// not on the curve, an r or s out of range, DER that does not parse — comes back 0b, because
// saying no is the whole job and a signal is an answer the caller has to remember to catch.
// Verification only: there is no signing here, and nothing is constant-time, which is what a
// verifier's all-public inputs allow.
//
// WHAT IS CHECKED, because each one missing is a real forgery:
//   * r and s are both in [1, n-1] — s = 0 would make s^-1 meaningless and r = 0 lets an attacker
//     drop the public key out of the equation entirely
//   * the public key is a point ON the curve, both coordinates canonically below p, and not the
//     point at infinity — an off-curve "key" lands in a group where the discrete log is easy
//   * the recovered R is not the point at infinity
//   * the final test is (R.x mod n) = r, with the reduction ACTUALLY DONE. R.x is a value mod p
//     and r a value mod n, and on both NIST curves here p - n is tiny against the field — 127 bits
//     against 256 for P-256, 190 against 384 for P-384 — so a signature whose R.x reaches n turns
//     up about once in 2^129 and once in 2^194 respectively. That is what makes a missing
//     reduction so easy to ship: every vector openssl will ever hand you has R.x < n already, and
//     the bare comparison agrees with the right answer on all of them. tests/data/p256-rx-ge-n.py
//     and tests/data/p384-rx-ge-n.py build the cases that do not agree, and tests/p256.nt and
//     tests/p384.nt assert them.
// Both curves here have cofactor 1, so every point on them is in the prime-order group and there
// is no separate subgroup check to do; on a curve with a cofactor there would be, and ecCurve does
// not take one because nothing here would know what to do with it.
//
// COMPRESSED POINTS (SEC 1 0x02/0x03) ARE REFUSED. RFC 5480 2.2 requires uncompressed support and
// makes compressed optional; nothing on the public web ships one in a certificate, and recovering
// y from x is a square root mod p whose sign choice is one more thing to get wrong in a verifier
// whose job is to say no. `pub` must be the uncompressed 0x04 || X || Y form or it is not a key.

// ---- the curve record. The parameters go in as the hex the standards print (`openssl ecparam
// -name <curve> -param_enc explicit -text` prints exactly them), and come out as bignum.nt limbs
// with b and G already in Montgomery form, because that is the form every field operation below
// multiplies in. a is not stored: it is p-3 on both curves here and ecDbl's fast doubling assumes
// exactly that, so a curve with any other a does not belong in this file at all.
//   `p `np `r2       the field, and what bnMontMul needs to reduce in it
//   `pm2 `nm2        p-2 and n-2, the Fermat exponents the two inversions run to
//   `n               the group order, which the final comparison reduces against
//   `b `gx `gy       the curve constant and the base point, in Montgomery form
//   `one             R mod p, the Montgomery form of 1, which is also the Z of an affine point
//   `oct             a coordinate's width in octets: 32 for P-256, 48 for P-384
//   `bits `limbs     the scalar width in bits, and the bignum limbs that holds
//   `dgst            the digest length in octets this curve's signatures carry: see the header
// Limb exactness, the constraint every bignum in this tree is written against: bnMontMul forms one
// column of a schoolbook product as min(na;nb) products of 26-bit limbs, each < 2^52, so a column
// stays under limbs*2^52 — 2^55.4 at 10 limbs (P-256) and 2^55.9 at 15 (P-384), both far under the
// 2^62 an i64 column has to stay inside, with bnMontMul's carry (< 2^33) room on top.
// One list argument, not six: a call written over several lines is one statement per line here,
// so `f[a\n  ; b]` would hand f an empty argument per newline, while a `( ... )` list spanning
// lines is exactly the list it looks like. Five hex strings do not fit on one line.
//   (p; n; b; Gx; Gy; digest octets)
load "src/neant/stdlib/ct.nt"

ecCurve: {[a]
  ph: a[0]; nh: a[1]; bh: a[2]; gxh: a[3]; gyh: a[4]; dgst: a[5]
  P: bnFromBytes unhex ph
  N: bnFromBytes unhex nh
  np: bnMontInv P[0]                                      // -p^-1 mod 2^26, for bnMontMul
  r2: bnMod[((2*count P)#0), 1; P]                        // R^2 mod p, R = 2^(26*count P)
  nb: count unhex ph
  k: `p`np`r2`pm2`n`nm2`b`gx`gy`one`oct`bits`limbs`dgst
  v: (P; np; r2; bnSub[P; ,2]; N; bnSub[N; ,2]
    ; bnMontMul[bnFromBytes unhex bh; r2; P; np]
    ; bnMontMul[bnFromBytes unhex gxh; r2; P; np]
    ; bnMontMul[bnFromBytes unhex gyh; r2; P; np]
    ; bnMontMul[,1; r2; P; np]
    ; nb; 8*nb; ceiling (8*nb) % BNB; dgst)
  k!v}

// ---- the field mod p. Every value below is a limb list in MONTGOMERY FORM (the stored limbs are
// x*R mod p), because that is the form bnMontMul multiplies in; efTo and efFrom are the only two
// places the distinction shows. bnMontMul, bnAdd and bnSub all return trimmed canonical limbs, so
// `~` is a valid equality on field elements and efAdd/efSub keep every value in [0, p).
efMul:  {[C;a;b] bnMontMul[a;b;C`p;C`np]}
efSqr:  {[C;a] bnMontMul[a;a;C`p;C`np]}
efTo:   {[C;x] bnMontMul[x; C`r2; C`p; C`np]}             // x -> xR
efFrom: {[C;x] bnMontMul[x; ,1; C`p; C`np]}               // xR -> x
efAdd:  {[C;a;b] bnAddMod[a;b;C`p]}
efSub:  {[C;a;b] bnSubMod[a;b;C`p]}
// a^-1 as a^(p-2): p is prime, so this is the inverse for every a but zero, and zero inverts to
// zero — which is only ever reached through a Z of zero, and ecToAffine refuses that first.
// Fermat rather than a binary extended Euclid: Euclid would take roughly 2*bitlen iterations of
// shifts and conditional subtracts, fewer elementary operations than the bitlen squarings here,
// but each iteration is an interpreted loop body with branches and bignum.nt has no signed
// representation to run it in. p256.nt's header counts what that would and would not buy.
efInv: {[C;a] efTo[C; bnModExpL[efFrom[C;a]; C`pm2; C`p]]}

// ---- the group, in Jacobian coordinates (X;Y;Z) standing for the affine (X/Z^2, Y/Z^3). The
// point of Jacobian is that neither the doubling nor the addition inverts: the one inversion a
// verification pays is at the very end, in ecToAffine, instead of once per ladder step — one
// inversion per scalar bit saved, and each one is a bitlen-squaring exponentiation.
// Z = 0 is the point at infinity; X and Y are then arbitrary and the doubling below leaves it
// alone, which is why no formula here needs a neutral-element special case of its own.
ecInf: {[C] (C`one; C`one; BNZERO)}
ecIsInf: {[P] bnIsZero P[2]}

// dbl-2001-b (hyperelliptic.org's EFD), 3M + 5S, which is the fast one only because a = -3:
//   alpha = 3(X-Z^2)(X+Z^2) is the factorisation of 3X^2 + aZ^4 that a = -3 allows, and it costs
//   one multiply instead of two squarings and a multiply.
ecDbl: {[C;P]
  if[bnIsZero P[2]; :P]
  d: efSqr[C;P[2]]                                        // delta = Z^2
  g: efSqr[C;P[1]]                                        // gamma = Y^2
  bt: efMul[C;P[0];g]                                     // beta = X*gamma
  al: efMul[C; efSub[C;P[0];d]; efAdd[C;P[0];d]]
  al: efAdd[C; al; efAdd[C;al;al]]                        // alpha = 3(X-delta)(X+delta)
  b4: efAdd[C;bt;bt]; b4: efAdd[C;b4;b4]                  // 4*beta
  x3: efSub[C; efSqr[C;al]; efAdd[C;b4;b4]]               // alpha^2 - 8*beta
  z3: efSub[C; efSub[C; efSqr[C; efAdd[C;P[1];P[2]]]; g]; d]     // (Y+Z)^2 - gamma - delta
  g8: efSqr[C;g]; g8: efAdd[C;g8;g8]; g8: efAdd[C;g8;g8]; g8: efAdd[C;g8;g8]
  y3: efSub[C; efMul[C; al; efSub[C;b4;x3]]; g8]          // alpha(4beta - X3) - 8*gamma^2
  (x3;y3;z3)}

// madd-2007-bl: Jacobian P plus AFFINE A, 7M + 4S — cheaper than the 12M + 4S a second Jacobian
// point would cost, which is why the two-scalar table below is converted to affine once up front.
// The two degenerate cases are the ones a verifier must not get wrong: equal points need the
// doubling formula (the addition would divide by zero and hand back (0;0;0)), and a point plus its
// own negation is the point at infinity.
ecMAdd: {[C;P;A]
  if[bnIsZero P[2]; :(A[0]; A[1]; C`one)]
  zz: efSqr[C;P[2]]                                       // Z1Z1
  u2: efMul[C; A[0]; zz]
  s2: efMul[C; A[1]; efMul[C;P[2];zz]]
  h: efSub[C; u2; P[0]]
  rr: efSub[C; s2; P[1]]
  if[bnIsZero h
    if[bnIsZero rr; :ecDbl[C;P]]
    :ecInf C]
  rr: efAdd[C;rr;rr]                                      // r = 2(S2 - Y1)
  hh: efSqr[C;h]
  ii: efAdd[C;hh;hh]; ii: efAdd[C;ii;ii]                  // I = 4*HH
  jj: efMul[C;h;ii]                                       // J = H*I
  v: efMul[C; P[0]; ii]
  x3: efSub[C; efSub[C; efSqr[C;rr]; jj]; efAdd[C;v;v]]
  t: efMul[C; P[1]; jj]
  y3: efSub[C; efMul[C; rr; efSub[C;v;x3]]; efAdd[C;t;t]]
  z3: efSub[C; efSub[C; efSqr[C; efAdd[C;P[2];h]]; zz]; hh]      // (Z1+H)^2 - Z1Z1 - HH
  (x3;y3;z3)}

// Jacobian -> (x;y) in Montgomery form, or () for the point at infinity. This is the one inversion.
ecToAffine: {[C;P]
  if[bnIsZero P[2]; :()]
  zi: efInv[C;P[2]]
  z2: efSqr[C;zi]
  (efMul[C;P[0];z2]; efMul[C;P[1]; efMul[C;z2;zi]])}

// a scalar as `bits` bits, most significant first — the order the ladder below consumes them in.
// `limbs` 26-bit limbs hold a few more bits than that (260 for P-256, 390 for P-384) and every
// scalar here is already reduced mod n < 2^bits, so the bits dropped off the top are zero.
ecBits: {[C;v] reverse (C`bits) # raze {(x shr til BNB) band 1} each bnPad[v; C`limbs]}

// Shamir's trick, the same shape as ed25519.nt's edMul2: both scalars are consumed together over a
// three-entry table, so one doubling serves both and a verification costs `bits` doublings rather
// than twice that. A1 and A2 are affine; the table entry for A1+A2 is built once and made affine so
// that every addition in the loop is the cheap mixed one. A1+A2 can be the point at infinity (an
// attacker is free to send Q = -G), and () is how that entry says so: adding infinity is a no-op,
// so the loop simply skips it.
ecShamir: {[C;b1;A1;b2;A2]
  T: (A1; A2; ecToAffine[C; ecMAdd[C; (A1[0];A1[1];C`one); A2]])
  R: ecInf C
  i: 0; n: count b1
  while[i<n
    R: ecDbl[C;R]
    j: (b1[i]) + 2*b2[i]
    if[j>0
      e: T[j-1]
      if[0 < count e; R: ecMAdd[C;R;e]]]
    i+:1]
  R}

// ---- k*A for a SECRET k. Everything above this line is for verification, where every input is
// public; this is the one path a signer takes, and it is a different problem.
//
// ecShamir branches on the scalar: `if[j>0; R: ecMAdd[...]]` does an addition for a one bit and
// nothing for a zero, so how long a multiply runs counts the ones in the scalar. For verification
// that is free speed. For signing it is the whole key — the scalar there is the nonce k, and a
// handful of k's bits recovers d outright, because the lattice attacks on biased ECDSA nonces need
// far less than a handful. ecdsaSign originally called ecShamir, which is to say the side channel
// arrived with the signing code and not with the curve arithmetic it borrowed.
//
// This is double-and-add-always: every bit costs one doubling and one addition, the addition is
// performed whatever the bit is, and a mask rather than a branch decides which of the two results
// survives. src/neant/stdlib/ct.nt holds the mask primitives and says why each is written the way
// it is; they are used here rather than restated so that `jitct`'s check of them covers this too.
// The cost is one addition per bit instead of one per set bit, so signing does about a third more
// work than it did.
//
// TWO CASES ARE ARGUED AWAY RATHER THAN COMPUTED, and the argument is the usual one. The addition
// formula is undefined when the two points share an x coordinate, which here means 2m*A = +-A for
// the prefix m of the scalar, i.e. 2m = +-1 mod n. For a nonce drawn as RFC 6979 draws it that is
// one chance in 2^255 at each step. Note what the failure would be: a wrong signature, which
// verification catches, and NOT a timing difference — there is no branch either way, so the
// negligible-probability argument is only buying correctness, never secrecy.
//
// WHAT IS STILL NOT CONSTANT-TIME, and it is worth being exact about. The field layer below is not.
// bnMontMul trims its own result, so an intermediate with a zero top limb makes the next multiply
// cheaper, and bnAddModL's conditional subtract is a branch on a value. The efCt* wrappers below
// pad every operand back to the curve's full limb width, which closes the first of those inside
// this loop; the conditional subtract remains, and it is a second-order channel on intermediate
// coordinates rather than a first-order one on the scalar's own bits. The first-order one is what
// this closes.

// the field operations at a fixed width. The padding is not tidiness: bnMontMul costs
// `count a` times `count b` and trims what it returns, so without this the cost of each step
// would depend on how many leading limbs the last step's value happened to leave zero.
efCtMul: {[C;a;b] bnPad[bnMontMul[a;b;C`p;C`np]; C`limbs]}
efCtSqr: {[C;a] bnPad[bnMontMul[a;a;C`p;C`np]; C`limbs]}
efCtAdd: {[C;a;b] bnPad[bnAddMod[a;b;C`p]; C`limbs]}
efCtSub: {[C;a;b] bnPad[bnSubMod[a;b;C`p]; C`limbs]}

// all-ones when every limb is zero, zero otherwise. bnIsZero answers the same question through
// bnTrim, which scans down from the top limb and so takes longer the more leading zeros it finds;
// asking it about a Z coordinate would be a clock on the scalar's leading zero bits, which is
// precisely what is being hidden. Limbs are non-negative, so their bitwise or is zero exactly when
// all of them are.
ecCtZeroMask: {[v] ctMask ctZeroBit (bor/)v}
// mask all ones picks P, zero picks Q
ecCtSelPt: {[m;P;Q] (ctSel[m;P[0];Q[0]]; ctSel[m;P[1];Q[1]]; ctSel[m;P[2];Q[2]])}

// dbl-2001-b again with the `if[bnIsZero P[2]; :P]` taken out. The formula does not need it: for a
// point at infinity (X:Y:0) it computes z3 = (Y+0)^2 - Y^2 - 0 = 0, so infinity doubles to infinity
// on its own and the early return was only ever saving the work.
ecCtDbl: {[C;P]
  d: efCtSqr[C;P[2]]
  g: efCtSqr[C;P[1]]
  bt: efCtMul[C;P[0];g]
  al: efCtMul[C; efCtSub[C;P[0];d]; efCtAdd[C;P[0];d]]
  al: efCtAdd[C; al; efCtAdd[C;al;al]]
  b4: efCtAdd[C;bt;bt]; b4: efCtAdd[C;b4;b4]
  x3: efCtSub[C; efCtSqr[C;al]; efCtAdd[C;b4;b4]]
  z3: efCtSub[C; efCtSub[C; efCtSqr[C; efCtAdd[C;P[1];P[2]]]; g]; d]
  g8: efCtSqr[C;g]; g8: efCtAdd[C;g8;g8]; g8: efCtAdd[C;g8;g8]; g8: efCtAdd[C;g8;g8]
  y3: efCtSub[C; efCtMul[C; al; efCtSub[C;b4;x3]]; g8]
  (x3;y3;z3)}

// madd-2007-bl with all three early returns taken out, because a formula with a branch in it cannot
// be part of a constant-time multiply. The one case that is actually reachable — P at infinity,
// which is every leading zero bit of the scalar — is put back with a mask in ecCtMul below.
ecCtMAdd: {[C;P;A]
  zz: efCtSqr[C;P[2]]
  u2: efCtMul[C; A[0]; zz]
  s2: efCtMul[C; A[1]; efCtMul[C;P[2];zz]]
  h: efCtSub[C; u2; P[0]]
  rr: efCtSub[C; s2; P[1]]
  rr: efCtAdd[C;rr;rr]
  hh: efCtSqr[C;h]
  ii: efCtAdd[C;hh;hh]; ii: efCtAdd[C;ii;ii]
  jj: efCtMul[C;h;ii]
  v: efCtMul[C; P[0]; ii]
  x3: efCtSub[C; efCtSub[C; efCtSqr[C;rr]; jj]; efCtAdd[C;v;v]]
  t: efCtMul[C; P[1]; jj]
  y3: efCtSub[C; efCtMul[C; rr; efCtSub[C;v;x3]]; efCtAdd[C;t;t]]
  z3: efCtSub[C; efCtSub[C; efCtSqr[C; efCtAdd[C;P[2];h]]; zz]; hh]
  (x3;y3;z3)}

// sb is the scalar as bits, most significant first, exactly as ecBits gives them; A is affine in
// Montgomery form, as C`gx`gy and ecPoint both are.
ecCtMul: {[C;sb;A]
  one: bnPad[C`one; C`limbs]
  Aa: (bnPad[A[0]; C`limbs]; bnPad[A[1]; C`limbs])
  R: (one; one; bnPad[BNZERO; C`limbs])
  i: 0; n: count sb
  while[i<n
    R: ecCtDbl[C;R]
    zm: ecCtZeroMask R[2]
    S: ecCtMAdd[C;R;Aa]
    S: ecCtSelPt[zm; (Aa[0]; Aa[1]; one); S]     // R was infinity, so the sum is just A
    R: ecCtSelPt[ctMask sb[i]; S; R]             // and the bit says whether to keep the sum at all
    i+:1]
  R}

// ---- the public key. SEC 1 2.3.4: 0x04 || X || Y, `oct` octets each. -> the affine point in
// Montgomery form, or () for anything that is not a key: the wrong length, a compressed or hybrid
// prefix, a coordinate at or above p (two encodings of one number is exactly the ambiguity a
// verifier must not have), or a pair that does not satisfy y^2 = x^3 - 3x + b.
ecPoint: {[C;b]
  nb: C`oct
  if[not (1 + 2*nb) = count b; :()]
  if[not 4 = `int$ b[0]; :()]
  x: bnFromBytes b[1+til nb]
  y: bnFromBytes b[(1+nb)+til nb]
  if[not 0 > bnCmp[x;C`p]; :()]
  if[not 0 > bnCmp[y;C`p]; :()]
  X: efTo[C;x]; Y: efTo[C;y]
  l: efSqr[C;Y]
  r: efAdd[C; efSub[C; efMul[C; X; efSqr[C;X]]; efAdd[C; X; efAdd[C;X;X]]]; C`b]
  if[not l ~ r; :()]
  (X;Y)}

// ---- the signature, in the DER both a certificate and a TLS CertificateVerify carry it in:
//   Ecdsa-Sig-Value ::= SEQUENCE { r INTEGER, s INTEGER }   (RFC 3279 2.2.3)
// der.nt does the strict part — a non-minimal INTEGER, a non-minimal length, a truncated element
// or a byte after the SEQUENCE all signal there — so this only has to reject a negative value and
// strip the 0x00 that DER puts in front of a magnitude with its top bit set. Curve-independent:
// the encoding of an ECDSA signature does not depend on which curve made it.
ecPos: {[b]
  if[127 < `int$ b[0]; signal "ec: a negative value in an ECDSA signature"]
  $[(1 < count b) & 0 = `int$ b[0]; 1_b; b]}
ecSigRS: {[sig]
  nd: derParse sig
  derWant[nd;DERSEQ;1b]
  k: derKids nd
  if[not 2 = count k; signal "ec: an ECDSA signature is exactly two INTEGERs"]
  (ecPos derInt k[0]; ecPos derInt k[1])}

// ---- ECDSA verification (FIPS 186-4 6.4.2). e is the digest as an integer; the truncation the
// standard asks for is the leftmost min(bitlen(hash), bitlen(n)) bits, and with the one hash each
// curve here is paired with that is the whole digest, so there is nothing to truncate — the
// reduction mod n below is the ordinary one every later step works under.
//
// ECDSA IS MALLEABLE: if (r,s) verifies then so does (r, n-s), because negating s negates both u1
// and u2 and so negates R, and -R has the same x. That is not a flaw this verifier can or should
// paper over — the signature still commits to the same message under the same key, and a
// certificate or a CertificateVerify is authenticated by exactly that. Anything that needs a
// signature to be a unique token of a transaction has to say so itself.
ecVerify: {[a]
  C: a[0]; pub: a[1]; digest: a[2]; rb: a[3]; sb: a[4]
  if[not (C`dgst) = count digest; :0b]                    // one curve, one hash: see the header
  rv: bnFromBytes rb
  sval: bnFromBytes sb
  if[bnIsZero rv; :0b]                                    // r and s both in [1, n-1]
  if[bnIsZero sval; :0b]
  if[not 0 > bnCmp[rv;C`n]; :0b]
  if[not 0 > bnCmp[sval;C`n]; :0b]
  Q: ecPoint[C;pub]
  if[0 = count Q; :0b]
  e: bnMod[bnFromBytes digest; C`n]
  w: bnModExpL[sval; C`nm2; C`n]                          // s^-1 mod n; n is prime, so Fermat
  u1: bnMod[bnMul[e;w]; C`n]
  u2: bnMod[bnMul[rv;w]; C`n]
  G: (C`gx; C`gy)
  R: ecShamir[C; ecBits[C;u1]; G; ecBits[C;u2]; Q]
  if[bnIsZero R[2]; :0b]                                  // u1*G + u2*Q is the point at infinity
  xy: ecToAffine[C;R]
  (bnMod[efFrom[C;xy[0]]; C`n]) ~ rv}                     // mod n, never a bare compare: see above

// The two exports. The trap is belt and braces over the explicit checks above: bignum.nt signals
// on an operand wider than it can multiply exactly, and a certificate does not get to turn that
// into an exception the caller has to catch. A signal from anywhere below is an answer of no.
ecdsaVerify: {[C;pub;digest;r;s] @[ecVerify; (C;pub;digest;r;s); {0b}]}
ecdsaVerifyDer: {[C;pub;digest;sig]
  rs: @[ecSigRS; sig; {()}]
  if[0 = count rs; :0b]
  ecdsaVerify[C; pub; digest; rs[0]; rs[1]]}
